/* book Worked example 2: Q: A zirconia cell runs at 800 °C with reference air (pO₂ = 0.21 bar) inside. Using the Nernst equation, find the cell voltage when the exhaust holds (a) 1 % oxygen, pO₂ = 0.01 bar (lean), and (b) an equilibrium oxygen pressure of 10⁻²⁰ bar (rich). Take R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) and F = 96 485 C/mol.
   1. Temperature: T = 800 + 273.15 = 1073.15 K.
   2. Factor: RT ÷ 4F = 8.314 × 1073.15 ÷ (4 × 96 485) = 0.02312 V.
   3. (a) ln(0.21 ÷ 0.01) = ln 21 = 3.045, so E = 0.02312 × 3.045 = 0.070 V.
   4. (b) ln(0.21 ÷ 10⁻²⁰) = ln(2.1 × 10¹⁹) = 44.49, so E = 0.02312 × 44.49 = 1.03 V.
   5. Read: a lean exhaust still holds free oxygen, so the two sides differ little and the voltage is tens of millivolts. Rich exhaust has almost none, so the ratio jumps by about 10¹⁸ across λ = 1 and the voltage jumps with it.
   6. Check: real sensors show about 0.8 to 0.9 V rich, a little below the ideal 1.03 V, because the electrodes do not reach full equilibrium.
   Answer: About 0.07 V lean and 1.03 V (ideal) rich: the logarithm of a huge pressure ratio is what makes the narrowband sensor switch. */
