/* book exam traps: High EGO voltage means rich, not lean. The narrowband output is about 0.9 V on the rich side, at λ less than 1 (for example λ = 0.9), and about 0.1 V on the lean side, at λ greater than 1 (for example λ = 1.1). | A narrowband EGO voltage is not proportional to AFR. Reading 0.45 V as a precise lambda value is wrong; the sensor switches and the ECU uses the switch. | A cold sensor gives no useful signal. Light-off and heater control are part of the sensor system; a heater open circuit sets a fault and forces open-loop fuel. | Lead and silicone poisoning reduce response. Do not confuse poisoning with a rich mixture or a faulty heater. | Wideband pump current is not the same as the Nernst voltage. The sensing cell is held near 450 mV; the pump current carries the λ information. */
