/* book Worked example 2: Q: A car at 50 km/h hits a barrier and the passenger cell stops in 80 ms. (a) Find the average deceleration in m/s² and in g. (b) A MEMS accelerometer has a proof mass of 2 µg (2 × 10⁻⁹ kg) on springs of total stiffness 2 N/m, with comb gaps of 2 µm. Find the proof-mass movement and the fractional change in one gap.
   1. Speed: 50 ÷ 3.6 = 13.89 m/s.
   2. Average deceleration: a = Δv ÷ Δt = 13.89 ÷ 0.080 = 173.6 m/s², that is 173.6 ÷ 9.81 = 17.7 g.
   3. Spring force balances the inertia force: k x = m a, so x = m a ÷ k = 2 × 10⁻⁹ × 173.6 ÷ 2 = 1.74 × 10⁻⁷ m = 0.174 µm.
   4. Fractional gap change: x ÷ d = 0.174 ÷ 2 = 0.087, so one gap shrinks by 8.7 % and the opposite one grows by 8.7 %.
   5. Since C = ε₀A ÷ d, one capacitance rises and the other falls by about 9 %; the circuit reads that difference.
   6. Check: the peaks of a real crash pulse are two to three times the average, which is why airbag sensors are specified for ±50 g or more.
   Answer: Average deceleration 173.6 m/s² (17.7 g); the proof mass moves 0.174 µm, changing each comb gap by 8.7 %. */
